# Syntax

A Loom document is Markdown with a few marks added. Everything Markdown already does still works — headings, emphasis, lists, tables, links — and the marks say what Loom should do with the text around them.

There are eight of them, and each does a single thing. Learn these and you can read any corpus.

## The marks

| mark | what it does |
|---|---|
| `---` | fences the frontmatter that opens a chapter |
| `#` | names a section, and gathers the sections beneath it when it sits higher up |
| `[file.ts]` | names the file a section's code belongs to |
| `{Tangle}` | labels a heading — what Loom should make of it, or what language it holds |
| `=>` | opens a code block |
| `~` | closes a code block and returns to prose |
| `::[a section]` | draws that section in, whole |
| `{{ name = value }}` | binds a value an anchor can draw into the code |

Two more constructs set a stretch of text apart. Three backticks in prose fence a quote: Loom shows its lines and reads none of them as marks, which is how this page quotes marks without invoking them. A box sets a block of prose apart from the text around it. A line of three colons opens it, with an optional name and optional braces, and a line of three colons alone closes it. No mark inside a box opens anything. A box that names no kind is a note, a remark for whoever opens the corpus next, and tangling drops it with the rest of the prose. A box of the `Chapter` kind points to the chapter its name titles.

```loom
::: Why a grid {Note}
The room is small enough to model as cells, so the game needs no engine.
:::
```

## Frontmatter

A chapter opens on a `---` fence. Inside it, a few fields say where the chapter sits and what it is made of.

```loom
---
Part IV: The format
Chapter 1: Syntax
Package: src/
Language: TypeScript
---
```

`Part` and `Chapter` place the chapter in the corpus, and they are what the navigation is built from. `Package` names where the chapter's code is written. A directory, ending in a slash, holds the files its sections name; a single file takes the chapter's code whole. `Language` declares what the chapter's code is written in. A chapter whose language is `Prose` holds prose and no code, so an arrow in it is an error, as it is in a section labelled `{Prose}`. These documentation pages are written that way.

## Sections

A `#` heading opens a section, and everything under it belongs to that section until the next heading.

A section's title is also its name. That name is how every other part of the corpus refers to it, so titles are not decoration — renaming a section renames the thing that draws it in.

## Parts

A part gathers chapters, and the frontmatter names it, not a heading. The chapter that opens a part names the part on its own line, as `Part IV: The format` does above. Every later chapter of the part names it by its numeral alone, before its own number and title: `Part IV, Chapter 2:` and the title.

Headings never gather anything. A `##` heading opens a section just as a `#` heading does, and its depth only shows a reader how the chapter is laid out.

## Naming a file

A heading may name the file its code belongs to, by putting the path in brackets:

```loom
## The parser [parser.ts]
```

Everything that section composes is written to `parser.ts`, under the chapter's `Package` directory. One chapter can fill several files, and several chapters can fill one.

## Labelling a heading

A heading may also carry a label in braces, which says what Loom should make of the section rather than where its code goes.

```loom
## The module {Tangle}

## Installing it {Bash}
```

`{Tangle}` marks the section that composes the one file its chapter names, so a chapter that writes a single file needs no bracket. A bracket alone is enough to name a file, and a heading never needs both. A language in braces switches that one section into another language, so a chapter of TypeScript can drop into a shell command or a block of JSON without leaving the document.

## Opening and closing code

The `=>` operator opens a code block. The `~` separator closes it and returns to prose. Between them the text is code in the chapter's language, and Loom treats it as opaque — it reads the marks, never the language.

```loom
The parser reads one line at a time.

=>

export const parse = (line: string) => line.trim()

~

Trimming first means the caller never has to.
```

Prose and code alternate as often as the explanation needs. A block is not a unit of compilation; it is a unit of narrative.

A block opened with `=>` also ends at the next heading. That suits most code, but some files hold lines that look like headings: a Markdown page, a shell script, a configuration file with `#` comments. Fence that code, the way Markdown fences any code. Three backticks go on the first line after the arrow, and three more where the code ends. Every line between the fences is code, `#` lines included, and the closing fence returns to prose without a tilde.

````loom
## The skill [SKILL.md]

=>
```
# Writing for the reader

## Put the actor in the subject
```
````

The tilde that closes a plain block stands alone at the start of its line, or with a space and some prose after it. A line that merely begins with a tilde, such as `~/bin` in a script, stays code. A fence that never closes is an error, because it would otherwise swallow the rest of the chapter. Code that holds three backticks of its own, such as a Markdown file with an example in it, opens with a fence of four, and only four close it.

## Anchors

An anchor draws another section in by name, and it is how a file gets assembled out of the pieces that explain it:

```loom
## The module [parser.ts]

=>

::[The tokens]

::[The parser]
```

Order on the page and order in the file are separate concerns. A section may be explained early and composed late, or the reverse, because the anchor decides where its code lands.

Tangling checks every anchor. Name a section that does not exist and the build stops and says so, which is what keeps a document and its files from drifting apart as the document is edited.

Inside a code block an anchor is always an anchor, fenced or not. To write one as an example rather than as a reference, as this page does throughout, put it in backticks or in a quote, and Loom leaves it as text.

## Warps

A warp binds a name to a value. It is written in the prose before a section's first arrow, and an anchor with the same name draws the value into that section's code. A quoted string lands without its quotes, so the code writes any quotes it needs around the anchor.

```loom
## The version [version.ts]

{{ version = "1.2.0" }}

=>

export const VERSION = '::[version]'
```

Where a section repeats a version number, a package name or a path, the warp names it once.

## Tangling

`loom tangle` walks the corpus, resolves every anchor and warp, and writes each file out. What it produces is ordinary source: no marker, no runtime, nothing of Loom left in it. A reader of the output cannot tell it came from a document.

`loom weave` renders the same corpus the other way, as a document to read. These pages are the result.
